# Clean, student-facing URL for the HeadSpa Mastery course.
#
# The physical file stays headspa-mastery.html (per CLAUDE.md: avoid
# renaming it -- renaming breaks every existing bookmark/link
# immediately, a rewrite doesn't). This file teaches Cloudflare Pages'
# edge how to (a) serve that file's content under a clean path without
# changing the browser's address bar, and (b) permanently send every
# other way of reaching that file to the one clean canonical URL, so
# bookmarks/search results consolidate instead of duplicate-content at
# three addresses.
#
# CRITICAL, EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED GOTCHA (found via `wrangler pages dev`,
# both locally reproduced and matching documented Cloudflare Pages
# behavior): Cloudflare Pages automatically strips the .html extension
# off every static HTML asset and serves a client-visible 301/308 to
# the extensionless URL whenever the .html path is requested -- and
# critically, this automatic redirect also fires on a 200 rewrite rule's
# DESTINATION if that destination still ends in .html. An earlier
# version of rule 1 below targeted /headspa-mastery.html directly, which
# made Cloudflare's own automatic redirect leak through the rewrite:
# requesting /head-spa-certification returned a visible 308 to
# /headspa-mastery (a third, unconfigured URL), defeating the entire
# point of the rewrite. The fix: point the rewrite at the EXTENSIONLESS
# path (/headspa-mastery, no .html) that Cloudflare already treats as
# that file's canonical serving path -- since the destination is already
# in its canonical form, no secondary redirect fires, and the rewrite is
# genuinely transparent. Confirmed locally: GET /head-spa-certification
# now returns 200 with the real page content, address bar unchanged.
#
# Ordering note: rules 1, 3, and 4 below all have different source
# paths, so file order between them doesn't matter for matching
# ("top-most wins" only applies among rules that share the same source
# path). Per Cloudflare's own docs, a 200 rewrite's destination is
# served directly and is NOT re-run back through this file -- confirmed
# locally, requesting /head-spa-certification does not loop through
# rule 4 even though rule 4's source path is rule 1's destination path.

# 1. Rewrite (200): keep /head-spa-certification in the browser's
#    address bar while serving the real course file's content. This is
#    a proxy-style rewrite, not a client redirect, so the browser's
#    actual request -- path AND query string -- reaches the edge
#    untouched and is what the browser keeps showing; the deep links
#    the app already relies on (?enter=1, ?cert=1, ?studentpreview=1)
#    keep working exactly as they do today. headspa-mastery.html's own
#    inline script reads window.location.search/pathname generically
#    and has no hardcoded "headspa-mastery.html" path check, so this
#    rewrite is transparent to it. Destination is deliberately
#    extensionless -- see the gotcha note above; do not "simplify" this
#    back to /headspa-mastery.html, that reintroduces the redirect leak.
/head-spa-certification /headspa-mastery 200

# 2. Trailing-slash variant of the clean URL -- redirect to the
#    canonical (no trailing slash) form instead of dead-ending, which
#    then matches rule 1 above.
/head-spa-certification/ /head-spa-certification 301

# 3. Legacy filename (with .html) now permanently redirects to the
#    clean URL.
/headspa-mastery.html /head-spa-certification 301

# 4. Legacy filename WITHOUT .html -- Cloudflare Pages exposes this as
#    a real, independently-fetchable 200 URL for any static .html file
#    (it's the same canonical path rule 1 rewrites to internally), so
#    without this rule a visitor could land on /headspa-mastery directly
#    and get a second, non-canonical 200 URL for the same content --
#    exactly the duplicate-content problem this whole file exists to
#    avoid. Redirect it to the clean URL like rule 3 does for the .html
#    variant. Verified locally this does not loop with rule 1: a rewrite
#    destination is fetched directly and never re-enters this file (see
#    the ordering note above), so rule 1's internal fetch of
#    /headspa-mastery is unaffected by this rule existing.
/headspa-mastery /head-spa-certification 301

# 4. Cadence Intro Preview (cadence-intro-preview.html) is an owner/dev-only
#    review surface -- its own header comment says so explicitly: no
#    course, no Supabase, no entitlement/auth, a mocked reply that never
#    calls a real API, and it is explicitly written to be safe to open
#    locally as a file:// URL for review. It has never been linked from
#    any real page (grep confirms only source comments reference its
#    filename, never a live <a href> or redirect). The real Cadence intro
#    students actually see is served a completely different way: it is
#    mounted inline inside headspa-mastery.html itself via
#    mountCadenceIntro() (see that file), which this rule does not touch
#    at all. `<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">` on the
#    preview page is not access control -- it only asks well-behaved
#    crawlers not to index a URL that remains fully fetchable by anyone
#    who finds or guesses it. Since this is purely a dev/preview tool with
#    no legitimate production audience, the narrowest fix is to remove it
#    from public deployment routing entirely rather than build a new
#    auth-gate for a page that was deliberately designed to need none.
#    NOTE: Cloudflare Pages' _redirects only accepts status 200, 301, 302,
#    303, 307, or 308 -- a 404 status here is invalid and Cloudflare
#    silently drops the whole rule (confirmed via `wrangler pages dev`,
#    which rejects it outright), so an earlier version of this rule using
#    404 did NOT actually block the page. Using 301 to /index.html instead
#    -- not a true 404, but it stops the page from being served at its own
#    URL to an ordinary visitor, production and previews alike. The
#    physical file stays in the repo for local/file:// review exactly as
#    its header comment describes -- only the deployed URL is redirected.
/cadence-intro-preview.html /index.html 301
